WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.851
Cells take in substances through their surface. To compare cells, we use cubes. A cube has six faces, and every face is a square. The surface area is the area of all six faces added together. The volume is the space inside.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:30.679
The surface area to volume ratio compares the two. A small cube has a larger ratio than a big cube. So substances diffuse to its centre more quickly. This is why small organisms do not need a transport system.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:46.712
Here is a cube with sides of three centimetres. Step one: one face is three times three, nine square centimetres. Step two: there are six faces, so the surface area is six times nine, fifty-four square centimetres.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:55.471
Step three: the volume is side times side times side. Three times three times three is twenty-seven cubic centimetres.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:07.869
Step four: divide the surface area by the volume. Fifty-four divided by twenty-seven is two. So the ratio is two to one. Area units are squared. Volume units are cubed.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:27.629
Some questions ask for the area of a circle. Area equals pi times r times r. Pi is about three point one four. The radius, r, is half the diameter. So a diameter of ten millimetres gives a radius of five. The area is seventy-eight point five square millimetres.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:46.777
Now a real exam question. A student puts agar cubes in acid. The acid diffuses in, and the cube turns colourless from the edge. The cube has a side of two millimetres. Calculate its surface area to volume ratio, in the form n to one. Three marks.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:55.201
One face is two times two, four square millimetres. Six faces: six times four is twenty-four square millimetres.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.811
The volume is two times two times two: eight cubic millimetres.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.965
The ratio is twenty-four to eight. Divide both by eight: three to one.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:10.414
Here is the full working. It is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:22.985
A common mistake is to use four faces, or to work out side times three instead of side cubed. A cube has six faces, and volume is side times side times side.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:32.019
Another mistake is to write the ratio upside down, as one to three. Also, for a circle, use the radius, not the diameter.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:52.158
Your turn. A teacher makes a model cell from a cube of jelly with sides of four millimetres. A dye diffuses into it from the outside. Calculate its surface area to volume ratio. Will the dye reach its centre faster or slower than in a two millimetre cube? Pause here and work it out.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:03:00.233
One face is four times four, sixteen square millimetres. Six faces: ninety-six square millimetres.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:17.035
The volume is four times four times four, sixty-four cubic millimetres. The ratio is ninety-six to sixty-four, which is one point five to one. That is smaller than three to one, so the dye takes longer to reach the centre of this bigger cube.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:28.949
So, every time: surface area is six times side times side. Volume is side cubed. The ratio is surface area divided by volume, written as n to one.
